<SECTION image-layout="right" background-color="#1A1A1A">
  <IMG class="accent-image" query="Critical mineral supply chain visualization showing lithium processing bottlenecks, cobalt mining operations, and rare earth dependencies with global production capacity data overlays">
  <DIV class="body">
    <H1><SPAN text-color="#FFFFFF">Critical Mineral Shortages Will Constrain Battery Production Through 2027 Despite Recycling Advances</SPAN></H1>
    <P><I><SPAN text-color="#E5E5E5">Global battery production capacity will exceed critical mineral supply by 40% in 2027, creating supply-demand imbalances that threaten electric vehicle and energy storage deployment targets. Lithium supply gaps reach 2.1 million tons annually by 2030, while cobalt shortages emerge in 2026 despite Democratic Republic of Congo production increases.</SPAN></I></P>
    <COLUMNS colWidths="[50,50]">
      <DIV>
        <H4><B><SPAN text-color="#FFFFFF">Lithium Processing Bottlenecks</SPAN></B></H4>
        <P><SPAN text-color="#E5E5E5">Lithium constraints reflect processing bottlenecks rather than resource scarcity. Identified lithium reserves total <SPAN text-color="#FF6B35">98 million tons</SPAN> globally, but refining capacity reaches only <SPAN text-color="#FF6B35">1.2 million tons</SPAN> annually in 2024. New processing facilities require 5-7 years development timelines, creating structural supply lags behind battery demand growth.</SPAN></P>
        <P><SPAN text-color="#E5E5E5">Australia and Chile dominate production with <SPAN text-color="#FF6B35">67% market share</SPAN>, concentrating geopolitical supply risk. The temporal mismatch between facility development and demand acceleration creates a critical vulnerability window through 2027.</SPAN></P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B><SPAN text-color="#FFFFFF">Recycling and Rare Earth Dependencies</SPAN></B></H4>
        <P><SPAN text-color="#E5E5E5">Recycling technology advances offer partial solutions but insufficient scale. Li-Cycle and Redwood Materials achieve <SPAN text-color="#00D4FF">95% material recovery rates</SPAN> from end-of-life batteries, but recycled content supplies only <SPAN text-color="#FF6B35">12%</SPAN> of new battery demand through 2030. Battery lifespans of 8-15 years delay recycling material availability.</SPAN></P>
        <P><SPAN text-color="#E5E5E5">Rare earth dependencies for permanent magnet motors create additional constraints. China controls <SPAN text-color="#FF6B35">87% of rare earth processing capacity</SPAN>, with no significant alternative supply sources operational before 2028. Mountain Pass and Lynas facilities provide limited diversification but insufficient scale to reduce Chinese dependence below 70% market share.</SPAN></P>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>